From 3abb0934f5a8a84d83a1f9cde0f2bd04c08b2a09 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: karfau <christian.bewernitz@bettermarks.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 23:07:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] CVE-2026-83619: anchored end-tag whitespace trim to prevent ReDoS (upstream 3abb093)
 (GHSA-x4fp-j954-r2f4)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The end-tag name trim used an unanchored global regex `/[ \t\n\r]+$/g`. On a name
shaped `whitespace-run + one non-whitespace char` (an end tag `</   …   x>`), the
engine extended `[ws]+` to the end from every start position and then failed the `$`
anchor, backtracking quadratically in the length of the whitespace run — a single
small crafted end tag stalled the event loop (~1.4 s at 64 KB). The trim is now
anchored (`/^([\s\S]*?[^ \t\n\r])?[ \t\n\r]*$/` -> `$1`), matching the whole string
once and capturing everything up to the last non-whitespace character, so it runs in
linear time with byte-identical output. 0.8.x-only; 0.9.x uses an anchored matcher
and never had this regex.

https://github.com/xmldom/xmldom/security/advisories/GHSA-x4fp-j954-r2f4
